introduction:
  bigQuestion: "What really happens inside a computer when you run a program?"
  overview: "This unit focuses on how computer systems execute instructions at hardware level. You will study CPU components, memory hierarchy, instruction-cycle behavior, secondary storage choices, and cloud service models."
  journey: "You will describe how the ALU, CU, registers, and buses interact; explain RAM/ROM/cache purposes; describe the fetch-decode-execute cycle; describe internal and external secondary storage; and describe SaaS, PaaS, and IaaS cloud services."

concepts:
  - title: "CPU architecture"
    icon: "fa-microchip"
    content: |
      <p>The CPU is a coordinated system of components, not a single block.</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>ALU:</strong> arithmetic and logic operations.</li>
          <li><strong>CU:</strong> control signals and instruction coordination.</li>
          <li><strong>Registers:</strong> fast temporary state (PC, MAR, MDR, ACC, IR).</li>
          <li><strong>Buses:</strong> address, data, and control communication paths.</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "Primary memory hierarchy"
    icon: "fa-memory"
    content: |
      <p>Speed, cost, and capacity are balanced across memory types.</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Registers/Cache:</strong> fastest, smallest, closest to CPU.</li>
          <li><strong>RAM:</strong> volatile working memory for active programs.</li>
          <li><strong>ROM:</strong> non-volatile firmware/boot memory.</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "Instruction execution"
    icon: "fa-rotate"
    content: |
      <p>Every program runs through the fetch-decode-execute cycle repeatedly.</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Fetch:</strong> retrieve instruction from memory.</li>
          <li><strong>Decode:</strong> interpret opcode and operands.</li>
          <li><strong>Execute:</strong> perform operation and update state.</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "Persistent storage and cloud"
    icon: "fa-cloud"
    content: |
      <p>Systems combine local storage choices with cloud service models.</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Secondary storage:</strong> SSD, HDD, flash, optical, NAS.</li>
          <li><strong>Cloud models:</strong> SaaS, PaaS, IaaS.</li>
          <li><strong>Key trade-offs:</strong> performance, cost, control, and scalability.</li>
      </ul>

terminology:
  - term: "ALU"
    definition: "CPU unit that performs arithmetic and logical operations."
  - term: "Control Unit"
    definition: "CPU unit that manages instruction sequencing and control signals."
  - term: "PC / MAR / MDR / ACC / IR"
    definition: "Core CPU registers used to track instruction addresses, memory access, and intermediate results."
  - term: "Address/Data/Control bus"
    definition: "Communication channels for addresses, payload data, and system control signals."
  - term: "RAM"
    definition: "Volatile primary memory used for active programs and data."
  - term: "ROM"
    definition: "Non-volatile memory storing firmware and startup routines."
  - term: "Cache (L1/L2/L3)"
    definition: "Small high-speed memory layers used to reduce average access latency."
  - term: "Fetch-Decode-Execute"
    definition: "The repeating instruction cycle used by the CPU to run programs."
  - term: "Secondary storage"
    definition: "Persistent non-volatile storage for long-term data retention."
  - term: "SaaS / PaaS / IaaS"
    definition: "Cloud service models defining different responsibility boundaries."

misconceptions:
  - mistake: "RAM and storage are the same"
    correction: "RAM is volatile working memory; storage is persistent long-term data retention."
  - mistake: "PC holds the current instruction"
    correction: "PC normally points to the next instruction to fetch."
  - mistake: "Cloud means no physical hardware"
    correction: "Cloud services run on real physical infrastructure in data centers."

